haikuwebkit 1.10.0: seeking a <video> lands where it is asked to (docs/design/webpositive-video-overlay.md §6, WP3)

Applies after haikuwebkit-1.10.0-video-overlay.patch. MediaPlayerPrivateHaiku:
- duration() is the longest track's. It was the audio track's, and MediaTime() (a valid zero) for a file without an
  audio track: HTMLMediaElement::seekTask() clamps the target to the duration, so every seek of such a video went to 0.
- maxTimeSeekable() is the duration (it was currentTime(): seekTask() moves a target into the seekable range, so a
  seek forward stopped at the current position).
- seekToTarget() puts the video track on the key frame before the target and the video thread decodes on from there
  to the picture at the target, without showing the ones between (paused or playing; a new seek meanwhile takes
  over). The audio track, which the ffmpeg reader also seeks to the video's key frame, is decoded on to the target.
  A failed seek rejects the promise.
- The end of a track keeps the track (it was set to null, so a loop's seek to 0 found no track): without an audio
  track the video's end is the end (timeChanged(), and currentTime() is then exactly the duration), so loop and
  "ended" work for video-only files.
- The destructor and load() no longer hold the media lock while cancelLoad() waits for the threads (its own rule):
  the video thread takes it once per picture while it decodes on to a seek's target, and would wait for it forever.
- WEBKIT_VIDEO_OVERLAY_LOG=1 also prints a "WVO seek" line per seek and per picture shown after it.

